<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="robots" content="noindex,nofollow"><title>Paper A · Answer guide</title><base href="/api/v1/ap-chinese/2027/a/file/"><link rel="stylesheet" href="paper.css"><body><main class="page teacher"><section class="hero"><div><p class="eyebrow">HSKKit · AP Chinese 2027 · Original practice</p><h1>Paper A · Answer guide</h1><p class="lead">55 MCQs + four free-response tasks. Speaking uses an independent music-research scenario; the official 2027 project packet is not yet released.</p><div class="links"><a href="index.html">Practice workspace</a><a href="student.pdf">Learner PDF · 简体</a><a href="student-traditional.pdf">Learner PDF · 繁體</a><a href="teacher.pdf">Answer PDF</a><a href="listening.html">Audio review</a></div></div></section><div class="box"><h2>Before speaking: prepare your own research</h2><p>Before starting the speaking section, investigate how a musical practice in a Chinese-speaking community connects people. Use at least two identifiable sources, distinguish documented facts from your own interpretation, and prepare brief notes in your own words. The two source leads below are starting points; consult the original pages and record any additional research. This is independent practice, not an official course-project submission.</p><p><a href="https://ich.unesco.org/en/RL/guqin-and-its-music-00061">UNESCO: Guqin and its music</a></p><p class="cn">古琴是七弦弹拨乐器。联合国教科文组织的介绍把古琴与文人传统、较亲密的演奏环境联系起来。可以继续研究：音乐在怎样的场合被聆听？这种场合如何影响演奏者与听众的关系？这里的文化解释应以资料支持，不能据此概括所有当代演奏。</p><p><a href="https://ich.unesco.org/en/RL/nanyin-00199">UNESCO: Nanyin</a></p><p class="cn">南音与福建闽南以及海外闽南社群的文化生活有联系。联合国教科文组织的介绍提到泉州方言演唱、器乐与歌唱形式，以及婚礼、节庆等社会场合。可以继续研究：语言与集体演奏如何维系地方经验？不同社群是否会赋予同一音乐形式不同意义？</p></div><section class="box task"><p class="eyebrow">Free response 1</p><h2>Project presentation</h2><p>Present what you learned about how a musical practice in a Chinese-speaking community connects people. Describe a specific cultural practice or product, explain its relationship to a perspective or value, and support your explanation with your research. Include a comparison or reflection and acknowledge a limit in your evidence. Speak in Chinese.</p><p class="cn">请介绍你研究的一种华语社群音乐实践怎样连接人与社区。描述具体的文化实践或文化产物，解释它与某种观念或价值的关系，并结合研究证据。作适当比较或反思，也说明证据的局限。请用中文作答。</p><div class="key"><h3>Illustrative response · not the only acceptable answer</h3><p class="cn">我研究的问题是，音乐怎样让人感到自己属于一个群体。我先比较了联合国教科文组织介绍的古琴与南音。资料说古琴有七根弦，并把它与文人传统和较亲密的演奏环境联系起来；南音则与闽南及海外闽南社群的生活联系密切，包含泉州方言演唱，也出现在婚礼和节庆等场合。
这些信息让我注意到，连接人与人的不只是旋律，还有聆听、演奏和使用语言的方式。较小的聆听环境可能让人更重视细节与交流；南音中的地方语言和共同参与，则可能使熟悉这些声音的人想起家乡。不过，“可能”是我的解释，不能说每位参与者都有相同感受。
和我熟悉的学校音乐活动相比，学校里常按一次演出的效果评价成功，而这些资料提醒我，还要看音乐怎样存在于日常交往和长期传承中。如果我组织交流活动，会先请参与者解释一首曲子对自己的意义，再决定怎样介绍给不熟悉它的人。
我的研究仍有限：这两页资料不是对所有当代听众的调查。我还想找演奏者和年轻参与者的访谈，看看他们怎样描述自己的体验。这样才能区分机构介绍、个人经验和我自己的判断。</p></div><ul><li>A specific practice and setting, supported by identifiable evidence.</li><li>A relationship between practice/product and a perspective, not a list of facts.</li><li>A supported comparison or reflection, with uncertainty acknowledged.</li><li>An organized, intelligible Chinese presentation.</li></ul><p class="muted">Manual analytic review · 11 points maximum: 任务完成 2, 文化理解 2, 组织 2, 语言控制 3, 表达呈现 2. No automatic grade.</p></section><section class="box task"><p class="eyebrow">Free response 2</p><h2>Project Q&A</h2><p>Respond in Chinese to four questions about your research. Each prompt is played twice, followed by a forty-second response window. The four responses form one task and are reviewed together.</p><div class="key"><h3>S2-1</h3><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/s2-1.mp3"></audio><p class="cn">你选择这个音乐实践作为研究对象，主要是想了解什么？请结合一个具体例子说明。</p><p class="cn">我主要想了解，音乐除了表演还有什么社会作用。例如南音资料提到泉州方言演唱和社区场合，这让我想到语言熟悉感与归属感的关系。我想继续核实，参与者自己是否也这样理解。</p></div><div class="key"><h3>S2-2</h3><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/s2-2.mp3"></audio><p class="cn">请说明你使用的两份资料分别帮助你了解了什么。它们还有什么不能说明的问题？</p><p class="cn">古琴资料帮助我了解乐器特点和文人聆听环境，南音资料说明地方语言与社会场合。但这两页都不能代表每位当代参与者的感受。我需要访谈等补充来源，也要分清机构介绍和个人经验。</p></div><div class="key"><h3>S2-3</h3><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/s2-3.mp3"></audio><p class="cn">你研究的音乐实践和你熟悉的一种音乐活动，有什么相似或不同？这种比较让你想到什么？</p><p class="cn">它们都可能通过共同参与连接人，不过学校演出常以一次舞台呈现为中心，社区音乐也可能和节庆、语言或长期交往相连。这个比较让我觉得，不能只按演出是否热闹评价音乐的价值。</p></div><div class="key"><h3>S2-4</h3><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/s2-4.mp3"></audio><p class="cn">如果要把这种音乐介绍给不熟悉它的人，你会怎样设计一次交流？为什么这样安排？</p><p class="cn">我会先请演奏者解释一个具体片段的背景，再让听众提问或尝试节奏。需要翻译时保留原来的声音，并说明补充解释来自谁。这样既方便理解，也避免把一种文化简化成几个陌生符号。</p></div><p class="muted">Manual analytic review · 9 points maximum: 任务完成 2, 文化理解 2, 语言控制 3, 流利度 2. No automatic grade.</p></section><section class="box task"><p class="eyebrow">Free response 3</p><h2>Story narration</h2><p>Write a complete story in Chinese using all four pictures in the order shown. Include a beginning, a development, and an ending. The pictures provide the situation; choose your own details and wording.</p><p class="cn">请按顺序使用全部四幅图，用中文写一个完整故事，包括起因、经过和结果。人物姓名与具体细节由你决定。</p><div class="story-grid"><figure><img src="assets/images/story-1.svg" alt="Volunteers prepare an outdoor book exchange; dark clouds approach."><figcaption>Picture 1</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="assets/images/story-2.svg" alt="Rain begins; a volunteer shields books on the table while another notices the community room."><figcaption>Picture 2</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="assets/images/story-3.svg" alt="Volunteers carry the boxed books into the community room together."><figcaption>Picture 3</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="assets/images/story-4.svg" alt="Neighbors exchange and read dry books indoors; the volunteers are relieved."><figcaption>Picture 4</figcaption></figure></div><div class="key"><h3>Illustrative response · not the only acceptable answer</h3><p class="cn">星期六上午，小林和朋友在社区门口摆好桌子，准备举行旧书交换。大家把书分成几类，还没等邻居们到齐，天空就暗了下来。
突然下起雨，小林赶紧用外套护住桌上的书，朋友则四处寻找可以避雨的地方。看到旁边的社区阅览室开着门，他们先向工作人员说明情况，得到同意后，把书装进纸箱，一起搬进屋里。
两人重新摆好桌子，在门口放上指路的牌子。邻居们陆续进来，有人拿出带来的书，也有人坐下翻阅。虽然活动换了地方，大家交流得比预想中还热闹。
看着没有淋湿的书，小林松了一口气。她明白，提前安排很重要，遇到变化时愿意合作、及时想办法，也能让原来的计划继续下去。</p></div><ul><li>Use all four pictures in sequence; do not merely list objects.</li><li>Connect the approaching rain, the problem, the cooperative move, and the indoor outcome.</li><li>Add plausible details without contradicting the pictures.</li><li>Use coherent Chinese with an appropriate narrative voice.</li></ul><p class="muted">Manual analytic review · 8 points maximum: 叙事展开 3, 组织 2, 语言控制 3. No automatic grade.</p></section><section class="box task"><p class="eyebrow">Free response 4</p><h2>Email response</h2><p>You received the email below from a community-center coordinator. Reply in Chinese, responding to all requests and using a suitable greeting and closing.</p><p class="cn">社区中心｜关于新居民交流活动
同学你好！我们想邀请你帮助策划一次让新搬来的居民认识邻里的活动。请推荐一种活动，说明为什么它适合不熟悉彼此的人，并介绍你可以负责什么。
有些居民不太熟悉中文，也有人只能在周末参加。你会怎样照顾这些需要？预算有限，如果不能购买新材料，你有什么替代办法？另外，请向我们提出一个你在准备前需要确认的问题。
谢谢！
协调员 林老师</p><div class="key"><h3>Illustrative response · not the only acceptable answer</h3><p class="cn">林老师您好！
谢谢您的邀请。我建议举办周末邻里地图交流会，请大家在一张简易街区图上标出自己喜欢的地方，再两三人一组介绍理由。刚搬来的居民可以借此了解环境，也有具体话题和别人交流。
我可以负责画底图、准备地点卡片，并在活动中协助分组。为了照顾中文不熟悉的居民，卡片可以配简单图标，也可以邀请愿意帮忙的双语志愿者解释。介绍时不要求每个人公开发言，愿意倾听的人也能参加。
时间建议安排在周六下午。如果没有预算购买材料，我们可以用干净的旧纸板画地图，请居民自带彩笔；没有彩笔的人可以共用。
准备前我想确认，场地是否有方便轮椅和婴儿车进入的通道？知道这个情况后，我就能更合适地安排桌椅。
期待您的回复！
许宁</p></div><ul><li>Recommend an activity and explain why it helps unfamiliar neighbors connect.</li><li>Describe your role and respond to language and weekend availability needs.</li><li>Provide an alternative when new materials cannot be purchased.</li><li>Ask one relevant preparation question; use an appropriate greeting and closing.</li></ul><p class="muted">Manual analytic review · 8 points maximum: 信息展开 3, 组织 2, 语言控制 3. No automatic grade.</p></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">listening · L1 · Questions 1–5</p><h2>Choosing a neighborhood activity</h2><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l1.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"><p>大家好，请看社区中心周末活动表。这里补充几条报名说明。旧物交换只收干净、能继续使用的小物件，不收现金，也不提供大型家具运输。自行车检查的预约已经满了，没有预约的居民可以到西门听安全讲解，但不能占用检查名额。阳台种植课还有位置，材料由中心准备，参加者请自己带一个空花盆。如果下雨，这节课改在一楼大厅，时间不变。王阿姨说，她星期六要照顾孩子，只能星期天下午来，而且想找一个可以慢慢坐下来聊天的活动，我建议她参加邻里故事会。故事会不要求提前准备演讲，带着愿意倾听的心就好。要提醒大家，表上的预约要求不会因为活动免费就取消。请根据自己的时间和需要选择，不要只看活动名称。</p></div><div class="cn"></div><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>周末社区活动安排</caption><thead><tr><th>活动</th><th>时间</th><th>地点</th><th>是否预约</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>旧物交换</td><td>周六 09:00–11:00</td><td>东院</td><td>不用预约</td></tr><tr><td>自行车检查</td><td>周六 14:00–16:00</td><td>西门</td><td>需要预约</td></tr><tr><td>阳台种植</td><td>周日 09:00–10:30</td><td>屋顶花园</td><td>需要预约</td></tr><tr><td>邻里故事会</td><td>周日 15:00–16:30</td><td>二楼阅览室</td><td>不用预约</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="muted">本安排及数据为原创练习情境。</p><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l1.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"><p>大家好，請看社區中心週末活動表。這裏補充幾條報名說明。舊物交換隻收乾淨、能繼續使用的小物件，不收現金，也不提供大型傢俱運輸。自行車檢查的預約已經滿了，沒有預約的居民可以到西門聽安全講解，但不能佔用檢查名額。陽臺種植課還有位置，材料由中心準備，參加者請自己帶一個空花盆。如果下雨，這節課改在一樓大廳，時間不變。王阿姨說，她星期六要照顧孩子，只能星期天下午來，而且想找一個可以慢慢坐下來聊天的活動，我建議她參加鄰里故事會。故事會不要求提前準備演講，帶着願意傾聽的心就好。要提醒大家，表上的預約要求不會因爲活動免費就取消。請根據自己的時間和需要選擇，不要只看活動名稱。</p></div><div class="cn"></div><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>週末社區活動安排</caption><thead><tr><th>活動</th><th>時間</th><th>地點</th><th>是否預約</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>舊物交換</td><td>週六 09:00–11:00</td><td>東院</td><td>不用預約</td></tr><tr><td>自行車檢查</td><td>週六 14:00–16:00</td><td>西門</td><td>需要預約</td></tr><tr><td>陽臺種植</td><td>週日 09:00–10:30</td><td>屋頂花園</td><td>需要預約</td></tr><tr><td>鄰里故事會</td><td>週日 15:00–16:30</td><td>二樓閱覽室</td><td>不用預約</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="muted">本安排及數據爲原創練習情境。</p></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>1. Which activity best meets Wang’s schedule and preference?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> The item exchange in the eastern courtyard.</p><p><b>B.</b> The neighborhood story gathering in the reading room.</p><p><b>C.</b> The bicycle inspection at the western gate.</p><p><b>D.</b> The balcony gardening lesson on the roof.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The chart places the story gathering on Sunday afternoon, matching both her availability and wish to talk.</p><p>表中故事会在周日下午，既符合她的时间，也符合坐下交流的偏好。</p><p>audio · 只能星期天下午来，而且想找一个可以慢慢坐下来聊天的活动</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> The exchange is on Saturday morning.<br>交换活动在周六上午。</p><p><b>C.</b> The inspection is on Saturday afternoon and concerns bicycles.<br>检查在周六下午，内容是自行车。</p><p><b>D.</b> Gardening is on Sunday morning, not afternoon.<br>种植课在周日上午。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>2. What changes if it rains during the gardening lesson?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> The lesson moves to Sunday afternoon.</p><p><b>B.</b> Participants must reserve a different session.</p><p><b>C.</b> The activity takes place in the upstairs reading room.</p><p><b>D.</b> The venue changes to the first-floor lobby.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Rain changes the room, not the scheduled time or reservation rule.</p><p>下雨只改变场地，时间与预约要求没有改变。</p><p>audio · 如果下雨，这节课改在一楼大厅，时间不变</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> The speaker explicitly keeps the time unchanged.<br>原文明说时间不变。</p><p><b>B.</b> A replacement reservation is not requested.<br>没有要求重新预约。</p><p><b>C.</b> The reading room belongs to the story gathering.<br>阅览室对应故事会，不是雨天种植课。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>3. What should a registered gardening participant bring?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> An empty flowerpot.</p><p><b>B.</b> A bag of prepared soil.</p><p><b>C.</b> A cash payment for materials.</p><p><b>D.</b> A set of gardening tools.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The center provides materials; participants provide a container.</p><p>中心准备材料，参与者自带空花盆。</p><p>audio · 材料由中心准备，参加者请自己带一个空花盆</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>B.</b> Prepared soil is not listed as something to bring.<br>没有要求自带土壤。</p><p><b>C.</b> The instructions do not request cash.<br>说明没有要求现金。</p><p><b>D.</b> The instructions specify a pot rather than tools.<br>明确要求的是花盆，不是工具。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>4. What can someone without a bicycle reservation do?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Join an inspection if they arrive before opening.</p><p><b>B.</b> Pay to obtain an additional inspection place.</p><p><b>C.</b> Attend the safety talk without taking an inspection place.</p><p><b>D.</b> Move their bicycle inspection to the gardening venue.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The talk remains accessible while booked inspections are protected.</p><p>未预约者可以听讲解，但不能占用已经约满的检查名额。</p><p>audio · 可以到西门听安全讲解，但不能占用检查名额</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> Early arrival does not replace a reservation.<br>早到不能替代预约。</p><p><b>B.</b> No paid extra places are offered.<br>没有提供付费加号。</p><p><b>D.</b> The rain plan applies to gardening only.<br>换场地的安排只针对种植课。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>5. Why does the speaker emphasize that the activities are free?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To announce that reservation requirements have been removed.</p><p><b>B.</b> To distinguish the price from the need to reserve some activities.</p><p><b>C.</b> To encourage residents to bring larger objects for transport.</p><p><b>D.</b> To indicate that the center will provide transport for exchange items.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A free activity may still require booking; the speaker corrects that possible assumption.</p><p>免费与是否预约是两件事，讲话者是在纠正可能的误解。</p><p>audio · 预约要求不会因为活动免费就取消</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> The speaker says precisely that requirements remain.<br>讲话者说要求仍然保留。</p><p><b>C.</b> Large furniture transport is not provided.<br>不提供大型家具运输。</p><p><b>D.</b> The speaker explicitly excludes large-item transport rather than offering it for free.<br>讲话者明确不提供大型物件运输，并非免费提供运输。</p></details></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">listening · L2 · Questions 6–10</p><h2>A river clean-up that changed direction</h2><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l2.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"><p>河边清洁队本月没有再公布“捡了多少袋垃圾”的排名，而是开始记录垃圾从哪里来。记者采访发现，连续三个周末，队员捡到的纸杯集中在步道入口，饮料瓶则多在下游弯道。一些人原先认为下游游客最不文明，但队员沿河观察后发现，有些瓶子是从上游漂来的，不能看见哪里堆得多就责怪哪里的人。清洁队与入口附近的摊位商量，先在两个周末试用可归还的杯子，押金退还点设在出口。为了比较效果，他们保留另一处入口原来的做法，同时记录两边的到访人数。队长提醒，人数不同，直接比较垃圾总量并不公平，还要看每一百位游客留下多少垃圾。几位队员担心记录数据会占用清洁时间。队长因此把工作分成观察和清扫两组，每周轮换。这个试验还没结束，队伍不会根据一次减少就宣布成功，而会检查杯子有没有真正被重复使用，以及退杯是否方便。</p></div><div class="cn"></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l2.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"><p>河邊清潔隊本月沒有再公佈“撿了多少袋垃圾”的排名，而是開始記錄垃圾從哪裏來。記者採訪發現，連續三個週末，隊員撿到的紙杯集中在步道入口，飲料瓶則多在下游彎道。一些人原先認爲下游遊客最不文明，但隊員沿河觀察後發現，有些瓶子是從上游漂來的，不能看見哪裏堆得多就責怪哪裏的人。清潔隊與入口附近的攤位商量，先在兩個週末試用可歸還的杯子，押金退還點設在出口。爲了比較效果，他們保留另一處入口原來的做法，同時記錄兩邊的到訪人數。隊長提醒，人數不同，直接比較垃圾總量並不公平，還要看每一百位遊客留下多少垃圾。幾位隊員擔心記錄數據會佔用清潔時間。隊長因此把工作分成觀察和清掃兩組，每週輪換。這個試驗還沒結束，隊伍不會根據一次減少就宣佈成功，而會檢查杯子有沒有真正被重複使用，以及退杯是否方便。</p></div><div class="cn"></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>6. What is the report mainly about?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Replacing all volunteers with an automated cleaning system.</p><p><b>B.</b> Ranking visitors by the amount of waste they produce.</p><p><b>C.</b> Testing ways to reduce waste by investigating its source.</p><p><b>D.</b> Closing a riverside path until every piece of litter is removed.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The team investigates patterns, pilots a change, and evaluates its effects instead of merely counting collected bags.</p><p>清洁队追查来源、试行措施并评估效果，不再只统计清扫袋数。</p><p>audio · 开始记录垃圾从哪里来</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> No replacement of volunteers is proposed.<br>没有提出替换志愿者。</p><p><b>B.</b> The team stops ranking bags and cautions against blaming visitors.<br>队伍取消袋数排名，也提醒不要随意指责游客。</p><p><b>D.</b> The path is not being closed.<br>没有关闭步道。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>7. Why is the concentration of bottles downstream not enough to identify who dropped them?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Some bottles may have floated there from upstream.</p><p><b>B.</b> The downstream bend has no space for visitors to stop.</p><p><b>C.</b> The volunteers remove all upstream rubbish before counting.</p><p><b>D.</b> The report says all drink containers are sold downstream.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The location where rubbish collects can differ from its point of origin.</p><p>垃圾聚集的位置可能不同于被丢弃的位置。</p><p>audio · 有些瓶子是从上游漂来的</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>B.</b> The report does not describe a lack of stopping space.<br>没有说明下游无处停留。</p><p><b>C.</b> Upstream waste is not said to be removed before counting.<br>没有说统计前清空上游垃圾。</p><p><b>D.</b> The selling location is not established.<br>原文未确定所有饮料的售卖地点。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>8. Which measure would make the comparison between the two entrances fairer?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Counting only bottles that volunteers personally saw being dropped.</p><p><b>B.</b> Adding the total number of rubbish bags from both entrances.</p><p><b>C.</b> Asking volunteers to clean the busier entrance for longer.</p><p><b>D.</b> Comparing the amount of litter per hundred visitors.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Accounting for visitor numbers avoids mistaking busier traffic for worse behavior.</p><p>按每百位游客比较，能减少客流差异对结果的影响。</p><p>audio · 还要看每一百位游客留下多少垃圾</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> That would omit rubbish whose disposal was not witnessed.<br>这样会漏掉未被目击丢弃的垃圾。</p><p><b>B.</b> Combining totals hides the comparison.<br>合并总量会掩盖两处差异。</p><p><b>C.</b> Longer cleaning changes collection effort rather than normalizing visitor numbers.<br>延长清洁时间不是按客流校正。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>9. How does the team address concern about time spent collecting data?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> It suspends rubbish collection for the rest of the month.</p><p><b>B.</b> It divides observation and cleaning between rotating teams.</p><p><b>C.</b> It limits observations to periods when no visitors use the path.</p><p><b>D.</b> It drops the comparison and returns to bag rankings.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The division allows both tasks to continue without assigning one role permanently.</p><p>分组轮换让两项工作都能持续，也不把一种角色固定给某些人。</p><p>audio · 工作分成观察和清扫两组，每周轮换</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> Cleaning is not suspended.<br>并未暂停清扫。</p><p><b>C.</b> The report introduces rotating roles, not a restriction to visitor-free periods.<br>报道安排的是分工轮换，不是限定无人时段观察。</p><p><b>D.</b> The team retains the trial.<br>队伍没有放弃试验。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>10. What attitude does the team take toward an early reduction in rubbish?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> It treats any reduction as proof of permanent success.</p><p><b>B.</b> It dismisses the trial because cup returns take effort.</p><p><b>C.</b> It regards the reduction as a result requiring further checks.</p><p><b>D.</b> It assumes returned cups must already have been reused.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The team also checks actual reuse and the convenience of returning cups before drawing a conclusion.</p><p>还要核查重复使用和退杯便利程度，才能判断效果。</p><p>audio · 不会根据一次减少就宣布成功</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> One reduction is explicitly insufficient.<br>原文明说一次减少不足以宣布成功。</p><p><b>B.</b> The trial continues rather than being dismissed.<br>试验仍在继续。</p><p><b>D.</b> Actual reuse is something to verify, not assume.<br>真正重复使用仍需核查。</p></details></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">listening · L3 · Questions 11–15</p><h2>Keeping an interview in the speaker’s voice</h2><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l3.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"><p>我们给交换生做的街区导览，是不是把受访居民的方言都换成普通话？不然大家可能听不懂。</p><p>我也担心听懂的问题，可是王爷爷说，有些称呼一换，小时候的感觉就没有了。他愿意再用普通话解释一遍，但不想让原来的声音消失。</p><p>那保留原音，后面加一段解释呢？不过时间只有六分钟，采访有半个小时。</p><p>我想选三小段，每段只说明一件事。第一段讲旧地名，第二段讲邻居怎么互相称呼，第三段讲他为什么还用这些说法。其余的可以放进文字补充。</p><p>这样会不会断章取义？他有一句话本来是在开玩笑，单独剪出来听着像在批评新居民。</p><p>你提醒得对。剪好以后，我们得请他听，确认上下文没有变。我也想把“这个地方以前叫什么”改成“这个名字让您想起什么”，也许能问出更具体的经历。</p><p>那导览开头呢？要不要说这种方言比普通话更真实？</p><p>别这样比较。两种说法各有用途。我们想让听众理解为什么一个人会在不同场合选不同的说法，不是给语言排高低。</p><p>好，我来做解释文字，你负责联系王爷爷确认剪辑。正式发布前，我们再找两位没听过采访的同学试听，看看他们能不能分清哪些是原话、哪些是我们的补充。</p></div><div class="cn"></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l3.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"><p>我們給交換生做的街區導覽，是不是把受訪居民的方言都換成普通話？不然大家可能聽不懂。</p><p>我也擔心聽懂的問題，可是王爺爺說，有些稱呼一換，小時候的感覺就沒有了。他願意再用普通話解釋一遍，但不想讓原來的聲音消失。</p><p>那保留原音，後面加一段解釋呢？不過時間只有六分鐘，採訪有半個小時。</p><p>我想選三小段，每段只說明一件事。第一段講舊地名，第二段講鄰居怎麼互相稱呼，第三段講他爲什麼還用這些說法。其餘的可以放進文字補充。</p><p>這樣會不會斷章取義？他有一句話本來是在開玩笑，單獨剪出來聽着像在批評新居民。</p><p>你提醒得對。剪好以後，我們得請他聽，確認上下文沒有變。我也想把“這個地方以前叫什麼”改成“這個名字讓您想起什麼”，也許能問出更具體的經歷。</p><p>那導覽開頭呢？要不要說這種方言比普通話更真實？</p><p>別這樣比較。兩種說法各有用途。我們想讓聽衆理解爲什麼一個人會在不同場合選不同的說法，不是給語言排高低。</p><p>好，我來做解釋文字，你負責聯繫王爺爺確認剪輯。正式發佈前，我們再找兩位沒聽過採訪的同學試聽，看看他們能不能分清哪些是原話、哪些是我們的補充。</p></div><div class="cn"></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>11. What solution do the speakers favor for listeners unfamiliar with the dialect?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Retain selected original audio and add explanations.</p><p><b>B.</b> Replace the resident’s speech with a student’s recording.</p><p><b>C.</b> Remove all language that needs additional explanation.</p><p><b>D.</b> Publish the entire interview without any editing.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>They aim to preserve the voice while making its meaning accessible.</p><p>保留居民声音，再用解释帮助听众理解。</p><p>audio · 那保留原音，后面加一段解释呢</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>B.</b> They do not agree to replace the speaker’s voice.<br>没有同意替换居民的声音。</p><p><b>C.</b> Unfamiliar expressions are part of the material’s value.<br>陌生表达正是材料有价值的部分。</p><p><b>D.</b> The six-minute limit requires selection.<br>六分钟限制要求筛选内容。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>12. Why should the resident hear the edited version before publication?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To make sure each excerpt uses standard Mandarin.</p><p><b>B.</b> To select the loudest parts of the recording.</p><p><b>C.</b> To confirm that editing has not changed the intended context.</p><p><b>D.</b> To decide which language is superior for a public tour.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>An isolated joke could sound like criticism; checking the cut protects the intended meaning.</p><p>玩笑剪出语境可能变成批评，回听是为了核对原意。</p><p>audio · 确认上下文没有变</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> Standard Mandarin is not the purpose of this review.<br>回听并非要求全部改用普通话。</p><p><b>B.</b> Volume is not the concern.<br>关注点不是音量。</p><p><b>D.</b> They reject ranking languages.<br>他们明确反对给语言排高低。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>13. What is the likely benefit of asking what an old place name brings to mind?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> It makes a factual list of names unnecessary for all future research.</p><p><b>B.</b> It invites the resident to connect the name with personal experience.</p><p><b>C.</b> It ensures the answer can be given without using any dialect.</p><p><b>D.</b> It directs the resident toward agreeing with the students’ opinion.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The revised open question seeks memories rather than only a label.</p><p>开放问题希望得到名字背后的具体经历，而不只是地名。</p><p>audio · 也许能问出更具体的经历</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> The question does not eliminate the value of factual research.<br>这种问题并未否定事实资料。</p><p><b>C.</b> It does not control which language the resident uses.<br>没有限制居民使用哪种语言。</p><p><b>D.</b> No preferred opinion is built into the question.<br>问题没有要求认同学生观点。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>14. Which statement best represents the male speaker’s view of dialect and Mandarin?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> A dialect should always replace Mandarin in public communication.</p><p><b>B.</b> Mandarin is suitable only for explaining unfamiliar place names.</p><p><b>C.</b> One language must be chosen before the interview can be recorded.</p><p><b>D.</b> Different forms of speech can serve different situations.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>His focus is why people make contextual language choices, not a hierarchy.</p><p>他关注不同场合的语言选择，不作高低评价。</p><p>audio · 两种说法各有用途</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> He does not advocate universal replacement.<br>没有主张一律替代普通话。</p><p><b>B.</b> He does not restrict Mandarin to one use.<br>没有把普通话限定为解释地名。</p><p><b>C.</b> The interview already exists in multiple forms of speech.<br>采访已经完成，不存在这个前置条件。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>15. What will the final trial listening help the students check?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Whether listeners distinguish the resident’s words from added commentary.</p><p><b>B.</b> Whether the resident remembers every street name used in the past.</p><p><b>C.</b> Whether the recording can be extended to thirty minutes.</p><p><b>D.</b> Whether two classmates can imitate the resident’s dialect accurately.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The trial checks clarity of attribution for an audience new to the interview.</p><p>试听检查不熟悉采访的听众能否分清原话与补充说明。</p><p>audio · 分清哪些是原话、哪些是我们的补充</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>B.</b> It is not a memory test for the resident.<br>不是测试居民的记忆。</p><p><b>C.</b> The six-minute format is not being extended.<br>没有延长六分钟格式。</p><p><b>D.</b> Imitating dialect is not requested.<br>没有要求模仿方言。</p></details></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">listening · L4 · Questions 16–20</p><h2>Contributing a reliable local observation</h2><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l4.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"><p>欢迎参加校园树木观察项目。今天我们先练习怎样留下能让别人复核的记录。进入页面后，请先确认地点，不要只按系统自动定位，因为两排树离得很近，定位有时会落到另一排。拍照时，先拍整棵树，再拍同一根枝条上的叶子；下次尽量从相同角度拍，才能比较变化。看不清树种时，可以选“暂不确定”，不要为了填满表格随便猜一个名字。照片里如果出现同学的脸，请重新取景，不要把未经同意的个人信息上传。遇到系统显示重复记录，也不要立刻删除。可能是两个人在不同时间看到了同一棵树。先比较日期、角度和备注，只有内容完全相同、又确定是误点提交时，才保留一份。本周每天午休开放十分钟提问，我们会一起看疑难记录。最后提醒，观察到没有开花，也是一条有用的记录；项目不是比赛谁找到的花多，而是建立连续的变化档案。</p></div><div class="cn"></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l4.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"><p>歡迎參加校園樹木觀察項目。今天我們先練習怎樣留下能讓別人複覈的記錄。進入頁面後，請先確認地點，不要只按系統自動定位，因爲兩排樹離得很近，定位有時會落到另一排。拍照時，先拍整棵樹，再拍同一根枝條上的葉子；下次儘量從相同角度拍，才能比較變化。看不清樹種時，可以選“暫不確定”，不要爲了填滿表格隨便猜一個名字。照片裏如果出現同學的臉，請重新取景，不要把未經同意的個人信息上傳。遇到系統顯示重複記錄，也不要立刻刪除。可能是兩個人在不同時間看到了同一棵樹。先比較日期、角度和備註，只有內容完全相同、又確定是誤點提交時，才保留一份。本週每天午休開放十分鐘提問，我們會一起看疑難記錄。最後提醒，觀察到沒有開花，也是一條有用的記錄；項目不是比賽誰找到的花多，而是建立連續的變化檔案。</p></div><div class="cn"></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>16. What should a participant do before relying on the automatically detected location?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Move to the second row of trees.</p><p><b>B.</b> Delete any earlier observations nearby.</p><p><b>C.</b> Turn off all location information in the project.</p><p><b>D.</b> Check that the location corresponds to the tree observed.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Nearby rows can be confused by positioning, so the location needs verification.</p><p>两排树很近，自动定位可能偏移，需要人工确认。</p><p>audio · 请先确认地点，不要只按系统自动定位</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> Moving rows would change the observation target.<br>移动到另一排不是核对地点。</p><p><b>B.</b> Nearby records need not be deleted.<br>不必删除附近记录。</p><p><b>C.</b> Location remains useful when checked.<br>地点信息经核对仍有价值。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>17. What is the purpose of photographing a branch from a similar angle on later visits?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To make all tree species appear identical.</p><p><b>B.</b> To make changes easier to compare over time.</p><p><b>C.</b> To avoid having to record the date of a visit.</p><p><b>D.</b> To prove the automatic location can never be wrong.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A consistent view improves comparison across visits.</p><p>一致视角有助于跨时间比较变化。</p><p>audio · 下次尽量从相同角度拍，才能比较变化</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> The goal is not to make species look alike.<br>目的不是让树种看起来相同。</p><p><b>C.</b> Dates remain important for comparing records.<br>日期仍是比较的重要信息。</p><p><b>D.</b> Photographic angle does not prove location accuracy.<br>拍摄角度不能证明定位绝对准确。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>18. What should someone do when they cannot identify a tree confidently?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Enter the most common species found on campus.</p><p><b>B.</b> Leave the project until an expert can visit in person.</p><p><b>C.</b> Mark the identification as uncertain rather than guess.</p><p><b>D.</b> Reuse the species name from an unrelated photograph.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The project allows uncertainty to keep the record honest.</p><p>允许标记不确定，是为了保持记录可信。</p><p>audio · 可以选“暂不确定”，不要为了填满表格随便猜一个名字</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> Popularity is not evidence for this tree’s identity.<br>常见程度不能证明这棵树的种类。</p><p><b>B.</b> The speaker provides an uncertainty option.<br>原文提供不确定选项，不必退出。</p><p><b>D.</b> An unrelated image cannot identify the observed tree.<br>无关照片不能替代鉴别。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>19. When should a record flagged as a duplicate be removed?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> When comparison confirms it is an identical accidental submission.</p><p><b>B.</b> Whenever it describes the same tree as any previous record.</p><p><b>C.</b> Whenever two different people submit observations on the same day.</p><p><b>D.</b> When the system notices the location is near another tree.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Repeated observations may be useful; only confirmed accidental copies should be consolidated.</p><p>同一棵树的连续观察有价值，只有确认误重复才合并。</p><p>audio · 只有内容完全相同、又确定是误点提交时，才保留一份</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>B.</b> Different dates or views can be useful observations.<br>不同日期或角度仍可能有价值。</p><p><b>C.</b> Two people may contribute distinct observations.<br>两个人可能提供不同观察。</p><p><b>D.</b> Nearby locations do not establish duplication.<br>位置接近不能证明重复。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>20. Why does the speaker mention a tree that has not flowered?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To discourage participants from visiting outside the flowering season.</p><p><b>B.</b> To suggest that incomplete records should be removed.</p><p><b>C.</b> To recommend recording only trees expected to flower soon.</p><p><b>D.</b> To show that absence of flowering is meaningful observation data.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The archive tracks change, including the absence of a visible event.</p><p>变化档案也需要记录某种现象尚未发生。</p><p>audio · 观察到没有开花，也是一条有用的记录</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> Visits outside flowering periods remain useful.<br>非花期观察仍然有用。</p><p><b>B.</b> A nonflowering observation is not incomplete.<br>未开花不等于记录不完整。</p><p><b>C.</b> The aim is continuous observation, not selecting only trees near flowering.<br>目标是连续观察，不是只选即将开花的树。</p></details></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">listening · L5 · Questions 21–25</p><h2>Repairing an object, keeping a memory</h2><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l5.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"><p>大家好，今天我想分享我们小组做的一项小调查：为什么有些旧物明明修起来比买新的贵，人们还是愿意留下？我们访问了六位邻居，没有把他们的选择简单分成节省或浪费。刘奶奶的收音机已经听不清新闻，却能让她想起以前一家人围坐的晚上。修理师傅把外壳换得太新，她反而不习惯。另一位受访者留下旧椅子，不是因为它有特别的故事，而是觉得修好以后还结实，扔掉可惜。这说明同样是“留下”，理由可以很不一样。我们原本准备办一个只教技术的修理日，后来改为先让带来物品的人说说，哪些部分可以换，哪些痕迹希望保留，再由师傅解释安全和成本限制。有的电器已经不适合继续使用，我们不会为了纪念而忽视危险，可以拍照记录，保留不通电的外壳，或者另找保存记忆的办法。调查只有六个人，不能代表所有居民的态度。不过，它改变了我们的提问：与其问“值得修吗”，不如先问“对您来说，什么值得留下”。</p></div><div class="cn"></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l5.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"><p>大家好，今天我想分享我們小組做的一項小調查：爲什麼有些舊物明明修起來比買新的貴，人們還是願意留下？我們訪問了六位鄰居，沒有把他們的選擇簡單分成節省或浪費。劉奶奶的收音機已經聽不清新聞，卻能讓她想起以前一家人圍坐的晚上。修理師傅把外殼換得太新，她反而不習慣。另一位受訪者留下舊椅子，不是因爲它有特別的故事，而是覺得修好以後還結實，扔掉可惜。這說明同樣是“留下”，理由可以很不一樣。我們原本準備辦一個只教技術的修理日，後來改爲先讓帶來物品的人說說，哪些部分可以換，哪些痕跡希望保留，再由師傅解釋安全和成本限制。有的電器已經不適合繼續使用，我們不會爲了紀念而忽視危險，可以拍照記錄，保留不通電的外殼，或者另找保存記憶的辦法。調查只有六個人，不能代表所有居民的態度。不過，它改變了我們的提問：與其問“值得修嗎”，不如先問“對您來說，什麼值得留下”。</p></div><div class="cn"></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>21. What is the presentation’s central point?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Older objects are always safer and cheaper than new ones.</p><p><b>B.</b> Decisions about keeping objects involve different kinds of value.</p><p><b>C.</b> A technical specialist should decide what every owner must preserve.</p><p><b>D.</b> Objects without a family story should always be discarded.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Sentimental, practical, and safety considerations shape different decisions.</p><p>情感、实用和安全等因素会形成不同的保留理由。</p><p>audio · 同样是“留下”，理由可以很不一样</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> The presentation gives cost and safety counterexamples.<br>原文提到维修更贵和安全问题。</p><p><b>C.</b> Owners are asked which traces matter to them.<br>物主的意愿也参与决定。</p><p><b>D.</b> The chair is kept for practical reasons, without a special story.<br>椅子没有特别故事，也因实用而留下。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>22. Why is Liu uncomfortable when the radio’s casing looks too new?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> She believes a newer casing makes the news harder to hear.</p><p><b>B.</b> She wants to sell the radio as a recently purchased model.</p><p><b>C.</b> She objects to all repairs regardless of their effect.</p><p><b>D.</b> The old appearance is connected to the memories she values.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Her response suggests that traces of age are part of the remembered experience.</p><p>旧外观所带的痕迹也是她所珍惜的记忆的一部分。</p><p>audio · 却能让她想起以前一家人围坐的晚上</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> The speaker does not link casing age to sound quality.<br>没有把外壳新旧与收音效果相联系。</p><p><b>B.</b> No sale is planned.<br>没有出售计划。</p><p><b>C.</b> Her objection concerns a particular change, not every repair.<br>她不适应某种改变，不是反对一切维修。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>23. How did the survey change the proposed repair day?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Owners would first discuss which changes and traces mattered to them.</p><p><b>B.</b> Only objects that could be repaired for free would be accepted.</p><p><b>C.</b> Repairers would restore every object to a new-looking condition.</p><p><b>D.</b> Participants would stop discussing costs and safety concerns.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The revised event places the owner’s priorities alongside technical advice.</p><p>调整后的活动把物主的偏好与专业建议结合。</p><p>audio · 先让带来物品的人说说，哪些部分可以换，哪些痕迹希望保留</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>B.</b> The speaker establishes no free-repair restriction.<br>没有规定只收免费能修的物品。</p><p><b>C.</b> The radio example questions automatic renewal.<br>收音机例子恰好说明不应一律翻新。</p><p><b>D.</b> Safety and costs are explicitly retained.<br>仍明确讨论安全和成本。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>24. What does the speaker suggest when an electrical object is unsafe to use?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Use it only during family gatherings to reduce the danger.</p><p><b>B.</b> Ask its owner to disregard all memories connected with it.</p><p><b>C.</b> Find a way to preserve its meaning without operating it.</p><p><b>D.</b> Let the age of the object determine whether safety rules apply.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Photographs or a disconnected casing can preserve memory without unsafe operation.</p><p>照片或不通电外壳可以保存记忆，同时避免危险使用。</p><p>audio · 另找保存记忆的办法</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> Occasional use does not address the stated danger.<br>偶尔使用也没有解决安全问题。</p><p><b>B.</b> The speaker still values the memories.<br>原文仍重视记忆。</p><p><b>D.</b> Age does not remove safety constraints.<br>年代不能取消安全约束。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>25. Why does the speaker mention the small number of interviewees?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To claim that six opinions are enough to represent everyone.</p><p><b>B.</b> To limit how broadly the survey’s findings can be generalized.</p><p><b>C.</b> To argue that interviews cannot reveal any useful information.</p><p><b>D.</b> To explain why all participants had the same reason for keeping objects.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The survey is useful for questioning assumptions but has a limited sample.</p><p>调查能帮助反思提问，但样本有限，不能代表全体。</p><p>audio · 不能代表所有居民的态度</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> The speaker explicitly rejects universal representation.<br>原文明说不能代表所有人。</p><p><b>C.</b> The survey already changed the group’s approach.<br>调查已经改变小组做法。</p><p><b>D.</b> The presentation emphasizes varied reasons.<br>报告强调理由不同。</p></details></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">reading · R1 · Questions 26–30</p><h2>A community music exchange</h2><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 上周征求意见时，几位社区居民说，他们很想听学生演奏，却担心从头到尾坐着听，自己没有参与的机会。我们因此把原定的舞台演出改成“音乐故事交流会”。活动仍在下周五下午举行，地点是社区活动室，不再使用学校礼堂。</p><p><b>p2</b> 前半小时，三位居民会带来对自己有特别意义的歌曲，讲讲第一次听到这些歌的经历；同学们可以提问。后半小时，由我们演奏短曲，并邀请大家试打节奏。陈阿姨愿意拉二胡，不过她希望先和学生见个面，商量怎样搭配，避免两边各演各的。</p><p><b>p3</b> 请大家在周三前选好一首不超过三分钟的曲子，并回复两三句话，说明为什么选它、想向居民问什么。我们不是要把旧歌照搬过来，也不是只追求新奇，而是想让不同的生活经历在音乐里找到联系。如果需要改编，请先问清楚歌曲的来源，以及带来这首歌的人是否介意。</p><p><b>p4</b> 另外，活动室离住户很近，不能使用扩音设备；预算也只够租几把椅子。请不要用报名人数或音量来判断活动是否成功。活动结束后，我们会请居民和同学各写下一件从对方那里听来的新鲜事。能不能真正听见彼此，比节目排得多热闹更重要。</p></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 上週徵求意見時，幾位社區居民說，他們很想聽學生演奏，卻擔心從頭到尾坐著聽，自己沒有參與的機會。我們因此把原定的舞台演出改成「音樂故事交流會」。活動仍在下週五下午舉行，地點是社區活動室，不再使用學校禮堂。</p><p><b>p2</b> 前半小時，三位居民會帶來對自己有特別意義的歌曲，講講第一次聽到這些歌的經歷；同學們可以提問。後半小時，由我們演奏短曲，並邀請大家試打節奏。陳阿姨願意拉二胡，不過她希望先和學生見個面，商量怎樣搭配，避免兩邊各演各的。</p><p><b>p3</b> 請大家在週三前選好一首不超過三分鐘的曲子，並回覆兩三句話，說明為什麼選它、想向居民問什麼。我們不是要把舊歌照搬過來，也不是只追求新奇，而是想讓不同的生活經歷在音樂裡找到聯繫。如果需要改編，請先問清楚歌曲的來源，以及帶來這首歌的人是否介意。</p><p><b>p4</b> 另外，活動室離住戶很近，不能使用擴音設備；預算也只夠租幾把椅子。請不要用報名人數或音量來判斷活動是否成功。活動結束後，我們會請居民和同學各寫下一件從對方那裡聽來的新鮮事。能不能真正聽見彼此，比節目排得多熱鬧更重要。</p></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>26. Which statement best summarizes the revised event plan?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> A student concert will raise funds for a larger performance space and a new set of instruments.</p><p><b>B.</b> Residents will judge student arrangements to decide which version best preserves the character of an older song.</p><p><b>C.</b> Students and residents will share performances and personal memories in an event designed to bring them together.</p><p><b>D.</b> Students will introduce new music while residents listen without taking part in the activities or discussions.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The revised plan combines residents’ memories, student performances, questions, and shared rhythm-making. Its central goal is connection through participation, rather than a one-way concert.</p><p>调整后的活动把居民分享经历、学生演奏、提问和共同打节奏结合起来，核心是通过参与建立联系，而不是单向观看演出。</p><p>p1 · 把原定的舞台演出改成“音乐故事交流会”</p><p>p2 · 三位居民会带来对自己有特别意义的歌曲，讲讲第一次听到这些歌的经历</p><p>p3 · 想让不同的生活经历在音乐里找到联系</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> The email mentions a limited budget, but it does not propose a fundraiser or new instruments.<br>原文提到预算有限，没有筹款或添置乐器的计划。</p><p><b>B.</b> No judging or competition is planned. Respecting a song’s meaning does not mean ranking versions.<br>活动没有评委或比赛，尊重歌曲意义也不等于评比不同版本。</p><p><b>D.</b> This repeats the passive format residents were concerned about, not the revised participatory plan.<br>这仍是居民担心的被动观看形式，与调整后的参与式安排相反。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>27. What should students include in their reply to Lin Yue?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Their chosen piece, the reason for choosing it, and a question they would like to ask residents.</p><p><b>B.</b> A resident’s favorite piece, a written account of its history, and permission from the music teacher.</p><p><b>C.</b> An estimate of attendance, a list of equipment needed, and a plan for covering the rental costs.</p><p><b>D.</b> A recording of their rehearsal, the names of all performers, and the order of the entire program.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Paragraph 3 asks students to choose a short piece and reply with why they chose it and what they want to ask residents. A includes all three requested elements without adding an unrelated requirement.</p><p>第三段要求选一首短曲，并回复选择理由和想向居民提出的问题。A 完整对应这三项内容，没有添加其他要求。</p><p>p3 · 选好一首不超过三分钟的曲子，并回复两三句话，说明为什么选它、想向居民问什么</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>B.</b> Residents share meaningful songs, but students are not asked to write a song history or get a teacher’s permission.<br>居民会分享有意义的歌，但学生不必提交歌曲历史或教师许可。</p><p><b>C.</b> Attendance, equipment, and budget appear elsewhere; they are not the requested contents of the reply.<br>人数、设备与预算出现在其他段落，不是这封回复要提交的内容。</p><p><b>D.</b> The email asks for two or three sentences, not a rehearsal recording or a complete program list.<br>原文要求回复两三句话，没有要求排练录音或完整节目单。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>28. In paragraph 2, what does “各演各的” most nearly mean?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Choosing musical styles from different generations to make the program more varied.</p><p><b>B.</b> Performing separately without planning how the two groups’ contributions will fit together.</p><p><b>C.</b> Playing the same piece more than once so that listeners can compare versions.</p><p><b>D.</b> Preparing their parts individually before combining them at a later shared rehearsal.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Chen wants to meet the students and discuss how their contributions can work together. The phrase describes the uncoordinated performances she wants to avoid, not the use of different styles or separate practice before a joint rehearsal.</p><p>陈阿姨想先见面商量如何配合。“各演各的”在这里指缺少共同安排、双方互不衔接地表演，不是音乐风格不同，也不是合作排练前的个人准备。</p><p>p2 · 先和学生见个面，商量怎样搭配，避免两边各演各的</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> Different generations and styles can still work together; the concern here is coordination.<br>不同年代或风格的音乐仍可以合作，语境强调的是缺乏协调。</p><p><b>C.</b> The phrase says nothing about repeating one piece or comparing its versions.<br>这句话没有重复同一首曲子或比较版本的意思。</p><p><b>D.</b> A later shared rehearsal would provide coordination. That does not capture the separation the phrase criticizes.<br>随后共同排练仍有协调过程，不能概括这句话所批评的各自为政。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>29. Which proposed addition would best fit the goals and limits described in the email?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Move to the school auditorium and spend the remaining funds on a longer student-only showcase.</p><p><b>B.</b> Use one familiar song for every performance so that the musicians can skip their planning meeting.</p><p><b>C.</b> Add amplified introductions so that neighbors who stay at home can also follow the activities.</p><p><b>D.</b> Let mixed groups discuss a song’s memories, then create a short rhythm quietly together.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>D applies several details together: exchanging personal experiences, active participation, rhythm-making, and keeping the event quiet. It proposes a new activity that fits both the purpose and the practical limits.</p><p>D 综合了分享生活经历、共同参与、打节奏和保持安静等信息。它是原文没有直接给出的新安排，但同时符合活动目标与场地限制。</p><p>p2 · 并邀请大家试打节奏</p><p>p3 · 想让不同的生活经历在音乐里找到联系</p><p>p4 · 活动室离住户很近，不能使用扩音设备</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> The school auditorium has been dropped, funds are limited, and a student-only showcase would reduce residents’ participation.<br>学校礼堂已不再使用，预算有限，学生专场也会减少居民参与。</p><p><b>B.</b> The email values planning together and connecting different experiences; requiring one song does not replace coordination.<br>活动重视共同商量和不同经历之间的联系，统一曲目不能替代协调。</p><p><b>C.</b> Amplification is explicitly prohibited because homes are nearby.<br>原文明确说明附近有住户，不能使用扩音设备。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>30. Why does Lin Yue ask students to check a song’s background and the resident’s feelings before changing it?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To decide whether the song is old enough to deserve a place in the final program.</p><p><b>B.</b> To ensure that changes respect the meaning the song has for the person who shares it.</p><p><b>C.</b> To prevent residents from hearing unfamiliar versions and keep the event entirely focused on older music.</p><p><b>D.</b> To make every performance follow the same arrangement so that its technical quality can be compared fairly.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Songs are connected to the residents’ personal experiences. Checking their background and asking before arranging them helps preserve respect for the people sharing those experiences; it does not ban creative changes.</p><p>这些歌曲联系着居民的个人经历。了解背景、改编前征求意见，是为了尊重分享者赋予歌曲的意义，并不意味着禁止创新。</p><p>p2 · 对自己有特别意义的歌曲</p><p>p3 · 请先问清楚歌曲的来源，以及带来这首歌的人是否介意</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> No minimum age for a song is mentioned. Personal meaning, not age, is the relevant concern.<br>原文没有歌曲年代门槛，关注的是个人意义，不是歌曲够不够老。</p><p><b>C.</b> Arrangements are allowed after discussion, and the event also includes students’ pieces.<br>原文允许先沟通再改编，活动也包含学生的曲目，并非只保留旧歌。</p><p><b>D.</b> The purpose is understanding and connection, not standardizing performances for technical judging.<br>活动追求理解与联系，不是把表演统一成便于技术评比的版本。</p></details></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">reading · R2 · Questions 31–35</p><h2>A library tries a different booking rule</h2><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 青禾图书馆试行座位预约后，管理人员发现，预约成功并不意味着座位被充分使用。有些读者一次预约三小时，实际上只坐半小时；也有人临时想来，却在页面上看见“已满”。管理员因此选了两个条件相近的阅览区，比较两种做法。</p><p><b>p2</b> 甲区维持原规则，每人最多预约三小时，迟到半小时才释放座位。乙区把单次上限改为九十分钟，迟到十五分钟释放；如果无人排队，读者可以续约。两个区域的座位数量和开放时长相同。下表是四周试行期间每天的平均数据，不是全市读者调查。</p><p><b>p3</b> 乙区的空置情况减少了，但满意度并没有随之提高。一些需要长时间阅读的读者说，反复续约让人分心。管理员打算保留较快释放空位的做法，同时增加少量可预约较长时间的座位。她强调，数据帮助我们看见问题，却不能替读者说明所有感受。下一轮除了记录数字，也会安排访谈，了解不同读者的需要。</p></div><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>两个阅览区的四周试行记录</caption><thead><tr><th>指标</th><th>甲区</th><th>乙区</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>每天平均预约次数</td><td>80</td><td>112</td></tr><tr><td>已预约时间中的空置比例</td><td>25%</td><td>10%</td></tr><tr><td>受访读者满意比例</td><td>78%</td><td>70%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="muted">原创模拟数据。满意比例来自各区试行期间的受访者，不代表所有读者。</p><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 青禾圖書館試行座位預約後，管理人員發現，預約成功並不意味着座位被充分使用。有些讀者一次預約三小時，實際上只坐半小時；也有人臨時想來，卻在頁面上看見“已滿”。管理員因此選了兩個條件相近的閱覽區，比較兩種做法。</p><p><b>p2</b> 甲區維持原規則，每人最多預約三小時，遲到半小時才釋放座位。乙區把單次上限改爲九十分鐘，遲到十五分鐘釋放；如果無人排隊，讀者可以續約。兩個區域的座位數量和開放時長相同。下表是四周試行期間每天的平均數據，不是全市讀者調查。</p><p><b>p3</b> 乙區的空置情況減少了，但滿意度並沒有隨之提高。一些需要長時間閱讀的讀者說，反覆續約讓人分心。管理員打算保留較快釋放空位的做法，同時增加少量可預約較長時間的座位。她強調，數據幫助我們看見問題，卻不能替讀者說明所有感受。下一輪除了記錄數字，也會安排訪談，瞭解不同讀者的需要。</p></div><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>兩個閱覽區的四周試行記錄</caption><thead><tr><th>指標</th><th>甲區</th><th>乙區</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>每天平均預約次數</td><td>80</td><td>112</td></tr><tr><td>已預約時間中的空置比例</td><td>25%</td><td>10%</td></tr><tr><td>受訪讀者滿意比例</td><td>78%</td><td>70%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="muted">原創模擬數據。滿意比例來自各區試行期間的受訪者，不代表所有讀者。</p></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>31. What problem prompted the trial?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Readers were reserving seats in a different city.</p><p><b>B.</b> The library had more seats than it could maintain.</p><p><b>C.</b> Readers were unable to access any reservation website.</p><p><b>D.</b> Reserved time was sometimes unused while others were excluded.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Unused reservations blocked other potential users.</p><p>预约未充分使用，却阻碍其他读者进入。</p><p>p1 · 实际上只坐半小时；也有人临时想来，却在页面上看见“已满”</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> The trial concerns two areas of one library.<br>试验是同一图书馆的两个区。</p><p><b>B.</b> Maintenance capacity is not the stated problem.<br>没有提及维护能力不足。</p><p><b>C.</b> The page is accessible and reports full bookings.<br>网页可访问，只是显示已满。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>32. Which conclusion is supported by both the article and the table?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Longer sessions always produce higher satisfaction for every reader.</p><p><b>B.</b> Better use of reserved time does not necessarily mean greater satisfaction.</p><p><b>C.</b> More reservations prove that the library needs fewer interviews.</p><p><b>D.</b> The new rules increased the percentage of reserved time left empty.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Area B has lower unused time (10% versus 25%) but lower satisfaction (70% versus 78%).</p><p>乙区空置比例更低，满意比例却也更低，利用率与感受不是同一指标。</p><p>p3 · 空置情况减少了，但满意度并没有随之提高</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> The data cannot establish an always-true rule for every reader.<br>不能据此推出对所有读者都成立的规律。</p><p><b>C.</b> The library plans interviews to understand the numbers.<br>管理员反而准备增加访谈。</p><p><b>D.</b> The unused proportion decreased, not increased.<br>空置比例是下降，不是上升。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>33. What is the difference between the two areas’ unused proportions?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> 15 percentage points.</p><p><b>B.</b> 15 percent of all library seats.</p><p><b>C.</b> 35 percentage points.</p><p><b>D.</b> 10 percent of all registered readers.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Subtracting 10% from 25% gives a difference of 15 percentage points in reserved time left unused.</p><p>25% 减去 10% 是 15 个百分点；分母是已预约时间，不是座位或注册读者。</p><p>chart · 25%</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>B.</b> The measure concerns time, not a count of seats.<br>该指标统计时间，不是座位数。</p><p><b>C.</b> Adding the two percentages does not measure their difference.<br>相加不能表示两者差值。</p><p><b>D.</b> The measure does not use registered readers as its denominator.<br>分母不是注册读者。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>34. How would the planned adjustment respond to the trial’s mixed results?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> By returning every seat to the original three-hour rule.</p><p><b>B.</b> By removing reservations and relying only on arrival order.</p><p><b>C.</b> By keeping faster release while offering some longer sessions.</p><p><b>D.</b> By requiring every reader to renew at fifteen-minute intervals.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The adjustment combines reduced blocking with an option for sustained reading.</p><p>调整同时兼顾释放空位和长时间阅读的需要。</p><p>p3 · 保留较快释放空位的做法，同时增加少量可预约较长时间的座位</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> The original rule is not being restored everywhere.<br>没有全部恢复旧规则。</p><p><b>B.</b> Reservations are retained.<br>预约制度仍保留。</p><p><b>D.</b> Fifteen minutes is the late-arrival limit, not a renewal interval.<br>十五分钟指迟到释放期限，不是续约频率。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>35. Why does the author describe the scope of the table’s data?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To show that every city follows the same reservation policy.</p><p><b>B.</b> To prove that interviews always give unreliable information.</p><p><b>C.</b> To demonstrate that the experiment lasted an entire year.</p><p><b>D.</b> To prevent readers from treating a local trial as a universal survey.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The limited place and period constrain how widely the findings apply.</p><p>地点与时间有限，不能把局部试行当作普遍调查。</p><p>p2 · 不是全市读者调查</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> No citywide uniformity is established.<br>没有证明全市规则一致。</p><p><b>B.</b> Interviews are proposed as useful additional evidence.<br>访谈是有价值的补充证据。</p><p><b>C.</b> The table covers four weeks.<br>表中记录的是四周。</p></details></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">reading · R3 · Questions 36–40</p><h2>A riverbank that leaves room for change</h2><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 青湾河边新修了一段步道，开放后却没有立刻得到一致好评。一位居民说，栏杆旁为什么还留着杂草和倒下的树枝？既然花钱改造，就应该收拾得整整齐齐。设计小组没有马上把这些东西清走，而是在现场组织了一次步行讨论。</p><p><b>p2</b> 生态调查员解释，规划中有一小段是“自然观察带”。一些枯枝给小动物提供藏身处，几种看似不起眼的草能留住土壤。保留它们不等于完全不管理：挡住通道的树枝必须移开，影响安全的地方也要及时处理。观察带与主步道之间留有清楚边界，方便人们选择行走路线。</p><p><b>p3</b> 居民并非都被说服。有家长担心孩子走进去，也有轮椅使用者指出，一处提示牌正好挡在坡道旁。小组原先准备在网上发一篇长说明，后来决定先移动提示牌，再请居民共同画出需要改进的路段。他们发现，有些争论源于对生态的不同理解，有些则是确实存在的使用障碍。</p><p><b>p4</b> 三个月后，观察带没有被变成花坛，但增加了简明标识，入口也更容易辨认。项目负责人说，公共空间不必在“全都人工整理”和“完全放任不管”之间二选一。让人理解保留的理由，同时愿意修改不方便的设计，才能让不同需要在同一条河边共存。</p></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 青灣河邊新修了一段步道，開放後卻沒有立刻得到一致好評。一位居民說，欄杆旁爲什麼還留着雜草和倒下的樹枝？既然花錢改造，就應該收拾得整整齊齊。設計小組沒有馬上把這些東西清走，而是在現場組織了一次步行討論。</p><p><b>p2</b> 生態調查員解釋，規劃中有一小段是“自然觀察帶”。一些枯枝給小動物提供藏身處，幾種看似不起眼的草能留住土壤。保留它們不等於完全不管理：擋住通道的樹枝必須移開，影響安全的地方也要及時處理。觀察帶與主步道之間留有清楚邊界，方便人們選擇行走路線。</p><p><b>p3</b> 居民並非都被說服。有家長擔心孩子走進去，也有輪椅使用者指出，一處提示牌正好擋在坡道旁。小組原先準備在網上發一篇長說明，後來決定先移動提示牌，再請居民共同畫出需要改進的路段。他們發現，有些爭論源於對生態的不同理解，有些則是確實存在的使用障礙。</p><p><b>p4</b> 三個月後，觀察帶沒有被變成花壇，但增加了簡明標識，入口也更容易辨認。項目負責人說，公共空間不必在“全都人工整理”和“完全放任不管”之間二選一。讓人理解保留的理由，同時願意修改不方便的設計，才能讓不同需要在同一條河邊共存。</p></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>36. What is the article mainly arguing?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Ecological preservation and usable public space can be negotiated together.</p><p><b>B.</b> A riverbank must be cleared completely before residents can visit.</p><p><b>C.</b> Professional explanations make public feedback unnecessary.</p><p><b>D.</b> A natural observation area should replace every accessible path.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The project keeps ecological features while responding to concrete access problems.</p><p>项目保留生态特点，同时解决实际通行障碍。</p><p>p4 · 让人理解保留的理由，同时愿意修改不方便的设计</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>B.</b> Complete clearance is rejected as the only option.<br>文章反对一律清空。</p><p><b>C.</b> Residents identify problems professionals need to address.<br>居民指出需要处理的问题。</p><p><b>D.</b> The main path and observation strip remain distinct.<br>主步道与观察带保持区分。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>37. Which action is consistent with the management of the observation strip?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Keeping every fallen branch regardless of where it lies.</p><p><b>B.</b> Replacing all wild plants with decorative flower beds.</p><p><b>C.</b> Moving a branch that blocks the walking route.</p><p><b>D.</b> Removing the boundary so that visitors must enter the strip.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Ecological retention is qualified by safety and access needs.</p><p>保留生态特征并不取消安全和通行要求。</p><p>p2 · 挡住通道的树枝必须移开</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> The text explicitly requires removing obstructions.<br>原文明说挡路树枝要移开。</p><p><b>B.</b> The strip is not converted to flower beds.<br>观察带没有改为花坛。</p><p><b>D.</b> A clear boundary gives users a choice.<br>清楚边界让使用者能选择路线。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>38. What does the misplaced sign reveal?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> All disagreement comes from a lack of ecological knowledge.</p><p><b>B.</b> Wheelchair users prefer longer online explanations to design changes.</p><p><b>C.</b> Signs are harmful wherever they are placed.</p><p><b>D.</b> Some complaints identify practical barriers rather than opposing conservation.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The sign is an access obstacle that a general ecological explanation would not fix.</p><p>提示牌构成实际障碍，单靠生态说明无法解决。</p><p>p3 · 有些则是确实存在的使用障碍</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> The article distinguishes misunderstanding from real obstacles.<br>原文区分误解与实际障碍。</p><p><b>B.</b> The proposed solution is to move the sign.<br>解决办法是移动提示牌。</p><p><b>C.</b> The problem is placement, not all signs.<br>问题在摆放位置，不是所有标识。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>39. Why did the team invite residents to map problem areas?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To transfer all maintenance responsibility to visitors.</p><p><b>B.</b> To gather specific feedback that could guide physical improvements.</p><p><b>C.</b> To prove that the original plan could never be changed.</p><p><b>D.</b> To identify complaints that could be settled simply by a longer online explanation.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Mapping makes experiences of the space actionable for redesign.</p><p>把使用体验落在具体路段上，能指导调整。</p><p>p3 · 请居民共同画出需要改进的路段</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> Responsibility is not transferred entirely to visitors.<br>没有把责任全部转交游客。</p><p><b>C.</b> The team has already begun changing the design.<br>小组已经开始调整设计。</p><p><b>D.</b> The mapping responds to physical barriers rather than treating every concern as a need for more explanation.<br>共同画图是为了处理实际障碍，不是把问题都当作说明不足。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>40. What does “二选一” refer to in the final paragraph?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Treating total tidiness and total nonintervention as the only alternatives.</p><p><b>B.</b> Choosing whether the river should serve children or older adults.</p><p><b>C.</b> Deciding whether to open the path in spring or summer.</p><p><b>D.</b> Selecting which resident will be responsible for all decisions.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The phrase challenges a false either-or framing of management.</p><p>这里反对把管理局限为全部人工整理或完全不管两种极端。</p><p>p4 · “全都人工整理”和“完全放任不管”之间二选一</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>B.</b> The text does not set age groups against each other.<br>没有要求在年龄群体间二选一。</p><p><b>C.</b> Seasonal opening is not the issue.<br>问题不是季节。</p><p><b>D.</b> No single resident is assigned all authority.<br>没有指定某个居民独自决定。</p></details></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">reading · R4 · Questions 41–45</p><h2>The folds of a paper map</h2><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 搬家那天，我在抽屉里找到一张折得发白的公交地图。手机里明明有更快的路线，我还是把它摊在桌上。外婆用红笔画过一条线，从旧家门口一直绕到河边，旁边写着三个小字：“别着急。”</p><p><b>p2</b> 小时候，我总嫌外婆坐车慢。她宁可多走一站，也要在市场前下车，看看熟悉的摊主，再沿河走回家。我问她为什么不坐到楼下。她说：“到了地方，不一定就算走完了路。”那时我不懂，只觉得大人总能给麻烦找到理由。</p><p><b>p3</b> 新家离公司近了，窗外却没有我认识的人。第一个周末，我按地图上的红线坐回去。市场换了招牌，卖豆腐的阿姨已经不在原处。我有点失望，直到一个收摊的老人问我，是不是以前常跟着外婆来的那个孩子。他记不住我的名字，却记得我总把书包带拖在地上。</p><p><b>p4</b> 回程时，我没有删掉手机里的最快路线，也没有照着旧地图走完每一个弯。我把那张纸重新折好，留出红线的一角。原来有些路不是为了省下几分钟，而是让一个人知道，自己曾经怎样被这个地方认出来。</p></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 搬家那天，我在抽屜裏找到一張折得發白的公交地圖。手機裏明明有更快的路線，我還是把它攤在桌上。外婆用紅筆畫過一條線，從舊家門口一直繞到河邊，旁邊寫着三個小字：“彆着急。”</p><p><b>p2</b> 小時候，我總嫌外婆坐車慢。她寧可多走一站，也要在市場前下車，看看熟悉的攤主，再沿河走回家。我問她爲什麼不坐到樓下。她說：“到了地方，不一定就算走完了路。”那時我不懂，只覺得大人總能給麻煩找到理由。</p><p><b>p3</b> 新家離公司近了，窗外卻沒有我認識的人。第一個週末，我按地圖上的紅線坐回去。市場換了招牌，賣豆腐的阿姨已經不在原處。我有點失望，直到一個收攤的老人問我，是不是以前常跟着外婆來的那個孩子。他記不住我的名字，卻記得我總把書包帶拖在地上。</p><p><b>p4</b> 回程時，我沒有刪掉手機裏的最快路線，也沒有照着舊地圖走完每一個彎。我把那張紙重新摺好，留出紅線的一角。原來有些路不是爲了省下幾分鐘，而是讓一個人知道，自己曾經怎樣被這個地方認出來。</p></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>41. Why does the narrator return along the red route?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To prove the phone’s directions are technically incorrect.</p><p><b>B.</b> To persuade all former vendors to reopen their stalls.</p><p><b>C.</b> To reconnect with a place associated with the grandmother.</p><p><b>D.</b> To reach the office faster from the new apartment.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The new home’s unfamiliarity motivates a return to a route filled with personal connections.</p><p>新家的陌生感促使叙述者回到承载与外婆共同经历的路线。</p><p>p3 · 我按地图上的红线坐回去</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> The narrator does not claim the phone is wrong.<br>叙述者没有说手机路线错误。</p><p><b>B.</b> No effort to reopen stalls is described.<br>没有游说摊主重新开业。</p><p><b>D.</b> The return takes place on a weekend and is not a commute.<br>周末回去不是为了上班更快。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>42. How did the child initially understand the grandmother’s slower route?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> As an unnecessary complication justified by adults.</p><p><b>B.</b> As the only physically possible route to their home.</p><p><b>C.</b> As evidence that the market was closed to younger people.</p><p><b>D.</b> As a way to avoid meeting familiar neighbors.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The child sees the detour as troublesome rather than socially meaningful.</p><p>小时候只觉得绕路麻烦，尚未理解其中的人际意义。</p><p>p2 · 只觉得大人总能给麻烦找到理由</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>B.</b> A direct stop near home was available.<br>当时可以坐到楼下。</p><p><b>C.</b> The market is not described as age-restricted.<br>市场没有年龄限制。</p><p><b>D.</b> Meeting familiar people is part of the grandmother’s reason.<br>见熟人正是外婆绕路的原因。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>43. What is the significance of the older man remembering the schoolbag strap?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> He can identify every resident by full name.</p><p><b>B.</b> A small remembered detail restores the narrator’s sense of connection.</p><p><b>C.</b> He wants the narrator to buy a new schoolbag at the market.</p><p><b>D.</b> He proves that the market has remained completely unchanged.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Being remembered through an ordinary habit gives the narrator recognition despite visible changes.</p><p>环境虽已变化，一个生活细节仍让叙述者感到被记住。</p><p>p3 · 他记不住我的名字，却记得我总把书包带拖在地上</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> He explicitly cannot recall the narrator’s name.<br>老人明确记不住名字。</p><p><b>C.</b> No schoolbag sale is proposed.<br>没有卖书包的情节。</p><p><b>D.</b> Signs and vendors have changed.<br>招牌与摊主已经改变。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>44. What does the narrator’s decision to keep both the phone route and the paper map suggest?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> The narrator intends to abandon the new home immediately.</p><p><b>B.</b> Only an exact recreation of the past can preserve memory.</p><p><b>C.</b> Paper maps are always more accurate than digital navigation.</p><p><b>D.</b> Efficiency and a meaningful connection to place can coexist.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The ending accepts a useful present without requiring the past to be reproduced exactly.</p><p>结尾既接纳现在的便利，也保留记忆，不要求原样复刻过去。</p><p>p4 · 没有删掉手机里的最快路线，也没有照着旧地图走完每一个弯</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> No immediate move is planned.<br>没有立刻搬走的计划。</p><p><b>B.</b> The narrator does not follow every old bend.<br>叙述者并未照走每个弯。</p><p><b>C.</b> The contrast is about value, not navigational accuracy.<br>对比的是价值，不是准确率。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>45. How does the meaning of “别着急” develop across the story?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> It becomes a warning that every bus on the route is unsafe.</p><p><b>B.</b> It turns into an instruction to finish moving more quickly.</p><p><b>C.</b> It comes to suggest allowing time for relationships and recognition.</p><p><b>D.</b> It means that the grandmother disapproved of every technological change.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The phrase grows from an instruction about pace into an invitation to notice social ties.</p><p>它从速度上的提醒，延伸为给人际联系和归属感留出时间。</p><p>p4 · 让一个人知道，自己曾经怎样被这个地方认出来</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> No bus danger is described.<br>没有公交危险情节。</p><p><b>B.</b> The phrase does not urge faster moving.<br>这句话并非催促搬家。</p><p><b>D.</b> The story establishes no blanket rejection of technology.<br>文章没有全面否定技术。</p></details></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">reading · R5 · Questions 46–50</p><h2>An invitation to repair, not replace</h2><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 青禾生活坊｜旧物新用体验日
周六 10:00–12:00，社区一楼工坊。费用为每人二十元，仅用于工具维护与基础耗材；不包括另购零件。十二个名额，请周四晚八点前预约。同行者如也要操作，须各自报名。</p><p><b>p2</b> 请带一件小型、非电器的旧物，例如开线的布袋、松动的木盒。我们不接收带电设备、尖锐破损玻璃或有化学残留的容器。不能确定物品是否合适？报名时附一张照片，工作人员会提前回复。未获确认的特殊物品请勿直接带来。</p><p><b>p3</b> 这不是把物品交给师傅、过一会儿来取的维修服务。导师会协助判断问题，参加者亲自尝试合适的步骤。没有经验也可以报名，但不保证每件物品当天修好。遇到需要专业设备的问题，我们会说明原因和后续选择，不勉强操作。</p><p><b>p4</b> 最后二十分钟是交流环节：请分享这件物品为什么值得留下，以及今天学到的一点经验。你也可以只倾听，不必公开私人故事。若临时不能来，请至少提前一天取消，让等候者有机会补位。</p></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 青禾生活坊｜舊物新用體驗日
週六 10:00–12:00，社區一樓工坊。費用爲每人二十元，僅用於工具維護與基礎耗材；不包括另購零件。十二個名額，請週四晚八點前預約。同行者如也要操作，須各自報名。</p><p><b>p2</b> 請帶一件小型、非電器的舊物，例如開線的布袋、鬆動的木盒。我們不接收帶電設備、尖銳破損玻璃或有化學殘留的容器。不能確定物品是否合適？報名時附一張照片，工作人員會提前回復。未獲確認的特殊物品請勿直接帶來。</p><p><b>p3</b> 這不是把物品交給師傅、過一會兒來取的維修服務。導師會協助判斷問題，參加者親自嘗試合適的步驟。沒有經驗也可以報名，但不保證每件物品當天修好。遇到需要專業設備的問題，我們會說明原因和後續選擇，不勉強操作。</p><p><b>p4</b> 最後二十分鐘是交流環節：請分享這件物品爲什麼值得留下，以及今天學到的一點經驗。你也可以只傾聽，不必公開私人故事。若臨時不能來，請至少提前一天取消，讓等候者有機會補位。</p></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>46. Who is the event primarily designed for?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Customers seeking guaranteed same-day electrical repairs.</p><p><b>B.</b> People willing to try repairing suitable small objects themselves.</p><p><b>C.</b> Professional repairers looking for unrestricted workshop access.</p><p><b>D.</b> Visitors who want to leave an object and collect it later.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>It is a guided hands-on learning experience, including beginners.</p><p>这是导师协助下亲自操作的体验，初学者也可参加。</p><p>p3 · 参加者亲自尝试合适的步骤</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> Electrical devices are excluded and success is not guaranteed.<br>排除电器，也不保证当天修好。</p><p><b>C.</b> It is a booked session, not unrestricted professional access.<br>活动需预约，不是自由使用工坊。</p><p><b>D.</b> Drop-off repair is explicitly distinguished from this event.<br>原文明说不是代修服务。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>47. A participant is unsure whether an unusual object is suitable. What should they do?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Bring it without notice so the tutor can decide during the session.</p><p><b>B.</b> Assume that paying the fee guarantees the object will be accepted.</p><p><b>C.</b> Ask a companion to register in their place without describing it.</p><p><b>D.</b> Submit a photo when booking and wait for staff confirmation.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Prior confirmation helps avoid bringing an unsuitable special item.</p><p>报名附照片并等待回复，能避免带来不适合的特殊物品。</p><p>p2 · 报名时附一张照片，工作人员会提前回复</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> Special items should not be brought without confirmation.<br>特殊物品未确认不应直接带来。</p><p><b>B.</b> Payment does not remove the eligibility rules.<br>缴费不能取消物品要求。</p><p><b>C.</b> Substitute registration does not assess the object.<br>换人报名不能判断物品是否适合。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>48. Which cost is not included in the twenty-yuan fee?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Basic consumable materials.</p><p><b>B.</b> Maintenance of shared tools.</p><p><b>C.</b> Additional replacement parts.</p><p><b>D.</b> Participation in the final discussion.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The notice explicitly excludes separately purchased parts from the fee.</p><p>通知明确说明另购零件不包含在费用中。</p><p>p1 · 不包括另购零件</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> Basic consumables are listed as covered.<br>基础耗材包含在费用用途内。</p><p><b>B.</b> Tool maintenance is covered.<br>工具维护包含在费用用途内。</p><p><b>D.</b> The final discussion is part of the session.<br>末尾交流属于活动本身。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>49. Why does the notice state that private stories need not be shared?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To allow participation without pressuring people to disclose personal experiences.</p><p><b>B.</b> To prevent participants from listening to anyone else’s account.</p><p><b>C.</b> To require every participant to submit a written history instead.</p><p><b>D.</b> To show that an object’s personal value has no place in the event.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The exchange invites meaning-making while preserving the choice not to disclose.</p><p>交流邀请分享意义，也尊重不公开个人经历的选择。</p><p>p4 · 你也可以只倾听，不必公开私人故事</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>B.</b> Listening is explicitly allowed.<br>原文明说可以倾听。</p><p><b>C.</b> A written substitute is not required.<br>没有要求书面历史。</p><p><b>D.</b> Personal value is invited, though disclosure is optional.<br>活动关注个人价值，但分享不是强制的。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>50. What is the stated reason for cancelling at least one day in advance?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To obtain additional parts at no charge.</p><p><b>B.</b> To allow someone on the waiting list to take the place.</p><p><b>C.</b> To transfer the booking automatically to every companion.</p><p><b>D.</b> To postpone the entire event to another weekend.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Advance cancellation makes a limited place available to another person.</p><p>提前取消能把有限名额让给等候者。</p><p>p4 · 让等候者有机会补位</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> Free extra parts are not offered.<br>没有免费零件安排。</p><p><b>C.</b> Companions must register separately.<br>同行操作者需要分别报名。</p><p><b>D.</b> One cancellation does not postpone the event.<br>个人取消不会延期整场活动。</p></details></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">reading · R6 · Questions 51–55</p><h2>Permission to publish an oral-history clip</h2><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 邮件一｜学生许宁致受访者周阿姨
周阿姨您好！谢谢您分享老街的故事。我们想把采访剪成三分钟短片，放在学校的公开网站上。片中会出现您谈到的旧门牌，以及您年轻时在街口拍的合影。我们准备只写您的姓，不写全名。请问您是否同意？如果有不适合公开的部分，请告诉我们。</p><p><b>p2</b> 邮件二｜周阿姨回复
许宁你好！我愿意让更多人了解这条街，不过照片里还有几位朋友，当年大家只答应私人留念，我不能替他们同意公开。请先不用那张合影。至于旧门牌，可以拍门牌本身，但不要拍到现在住户的门内。我的声音可以保留，最好先把剪好的片子发给我听一遍。有一句话是转述别人的猜测，不能剪得像是我亲眼见过的事。只写姓没问题，不过我希望页面说明这是个人回忆，不是经过全面核实的街道历史。</p></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 郵件一｜學生許寧致受訪者周阿姨
周阿姨您好！謝謝您分享老街的故事。我們想把採訪剪成三分鐘短片，放在學校的公開網站上。片中會出現您談到的舊門牌，以及您年輕時在街口拍的合影。我們準備只寫您的姓，不寫全名。請問您是否同意？如果有不適合公開的部分，請告訴我們。</p><p><b>p2</b> 郵件二｜周阿姨回覆
許寧你好！我願意讓更多人瞭解這條街，不過照片裏還有幾位朋友，當年大家只答應私人留念，我不能替他們同意公開。請先不用那張合影。至於舊門牌，可以拍門牌本身，但不要拍到現在住戶的門內。我的聲音可以保留，最好先把剪好的片子發給我聽一遍。有一句話是轉述別人的猜測，不能剪得像是我親眼見過的事。只寫姓沒問題，不過我希望頁面說明這是個人回憶，不是經過全面覈實的街道歷史。</p></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>51. Which use has Zhou explicitly declined for now?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Publishing the old group photograph.</p><p><b>B.</b> Including a recording of her voice.</p><p><b>C.</b> Showing the old street number by itself.</p><p><b>D.</b> Identifying her only by her surname.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>She cannot give public-release permission on behalf of the other people in the photograph.</p><p>她不能代照片中的其他人同意公开，所以暂时不用合影。</p><p>p2 · 请先不用那张合影</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>B.</b> She permits her voice, subject to reviewing the edit.<br>她同意保留声音，但要先听剪辑。</p><p><b>C.</b> She allows the number itself, without filming inside the current home.<br>她允许拍门牌本身，不拍门内。</p><p><b>D.</b> She accepts surname-only identification.<br>她接受只写姓。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>52. Why does Zhou want the edited clip sent to her first?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To remove every statement that differs from a published history book.</p><p><b>B.</b> To replace the entire interview with a written transcript.</p><p><b>C.</b> To check that a secondhand guess is not presented as firsthand evidence.</p><p><b>D.</b> To decide which school should host the public website.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Her concern is that editing could change the evidential status of a statement.</p><p>她担心剪辑把转述猜测变成亲眼所见，改变证据性质。</p><p>p2 · 不能剪得像是我亲眼见过的事</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> No requirement to match a history book is stated.<br>没有要求与历史书逐句一致。</p><p><b>B.</b> She allows voice use, so replacement is unnecessary.<br>她允许使用声音，不要求全部替换。</p><p><b>D.</b> The school website is already specified.<br>学校网站已经确定。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>53. What distinction does Zhou make about consent?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> A surname alone gives permission to publish any associated image.</p><p><b>B.</b> A student project never needs permission from anyone pictured.</p><p><b>C.</b> Permission for a private photo automatically includes public distribution.</p><p><b>D.</b> Her agreement does not authorize publication on behalf of other people.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Consent belongs to the people involved; she cannot extend her own permission to friends.</p><p>同意权涉及相关个人，她不能把自己的同意扩展到朋友身上。</p><p>p2 · 我不能替他们同意公开</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> Surname-only identification does not settle photo consent.<br>只写姓不能解决合影授权。</p><p><b>B.</b> Student status does not remove the issue raised in the email.<br>学生项目身份不能取消邮件提出的问题。</p><p><b>C.</b> She explicitly distinguishes private remembrance from publication.<br>她明确区分私人留念与公开。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>54. Which description would best meet Zhou’s requested framing of the clip?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> A complete and verified history of every family in the street.</p><p><b>B.</b> One resident’s recollections, with uncertainty clearly identified.</p><p><b>C.</b> An anonymous official record requiring no further context.</p><p><b>D.</b> A reconstruction in which every secondhand account is treated as fact.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The framing must preserve the limits of personal memory and identify uncertainty.</p><p>应明确个人回忆的范围，并保留不确定信息的标记。</p><p>p2 · 这是个人回忆，不是经过全面核实的街道历史</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> She rejects the claim of a fully verified history.<br>她不接受全面核实历史的表述。</p><p><b>C.</b> The interview is not an official anonymous record.<br>采访不是匿名官方档案。</p><p><b>D.</b> Secondhand guesses must not become established facts.<br>转述猜测不能当成确定事实。</p></details></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>55. How would you best describe Zhou’s overall response?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> She rejects every form of participation in the project.</p><p><b>B.</b> She accepts all proposed uses without any conditions.</p><p><b>C.</b> She supports sharing while setting specific boundaries.</p><p><b>D.</b> She agrees only if the website remains permanently private.</p></div><div class="key"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>She welcomes the project but specifies limits on images, editing, privacy, and attribution.</p><p>她支持分享，同时对图像、剪辑、隐私和说明提出边界。</p><p>p2 · 我愿意让更多人了解这条街，不过</p><details><summary>Other options</summary><p><b>A.</b> She permits several uses.<br>她允许多项使用方式。</p><p><b>B.</b> Her response contains several clear conditions.<br>回复有多项明确条件。</p><p><b>D.</b> She does not demand that the whole site remain private.<br>她没有要求整个网站始终私密。</p></details></div></article></section></main></body></html>